{
  "id": "Compound::DB01177 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Idarubicin, a蒽醌类化合物, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Breast Cancer through a series of mechanistic links that converge on the disease. The evidence suggests that Idarubicin may contribute to the development of Breast Cancer via the induction of Neutropenic Enterocolitis or Myelodysplastic Syndrome, both of which are associated with other drugs such as Paclitaxel and Thiotepa. These drugs are known to treat Breast Cancer, indicating a potential pathway where Idarubicin could act as a mediator in the progression of the disease. The shared ancestry with Irinotecan, a topoisomerase inhibitor, further supports a common mechanism of action, suggesting that Idarubicin may interact with cellular processes that are critical for Breast Cancer development. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Idarubicin to influence the disease through a combination of molecular and systemic effects, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
